WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.620
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello,
I'm Dustin Kirkland,

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.890
a product manager at Google.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.390
I'm here with Tim Hockin, tech
lead for the Kubernetes project

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.260
at Google.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.280
Tim, you've been around
Kubernetes since the beginning.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.870
You've seen the
past, the present,

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.170
and maybe have some
ideas about the future.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.210
Walk us through the
past, present, and future

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.890
of Kubernetes.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.220
TIM HOCKIN: Sure.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.375
So know Kubernetes was
born at Google, obviously.

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:24.900
It took a lot of
ideas from Google,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.770
but it is not exclusively
a Google thing.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.520
This community is an amazing
thing that has happened.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.950
The past, you know,
we've spent five years

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.060
building this system.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.610
We're here now with
8,000 of my best friends

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.230
talking about Kubernetes.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.563
It's amazing to
have gotten here.

00:00:42.563 --> 00:00:43.980
Five years ago, I
would never have

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.030
believed that we would be here
in Spain talking about this.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.805
Here we are today, we're
still working in Kubernetes.

00:00:50.805 --> 00:00:52.080
It is still maturing.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.050
It is servicing way
more than we initially

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.660
thought we would possibly
be able to address, more use

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.300
cases, more users, more scale.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.272
And future-- you know,
it's still growing.

00:01:03.272 --> 00:01:04.230
We're still doing more.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.010
We're tackling new
use cases every day.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.060
We've got customers and
users here all the time

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.190
talking about this new use
case, this new problem,

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.910
and so I'm excited to see
how much more we're going

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.083
to grow and tackle what's new.

00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:19.500
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And Google is one

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.125
of the leaders in the Kubernetes
project, but not the only one.

00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:23.790
There's a lot of
vendors involved

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.060
in and around Kubernetes
and other projects.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.550
How do you see the
role of vendors

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.560
in and around the
Kubernetes community?

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.790
TIM HOCKIN: I'm one of the
tech leads of the project.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.040
I work on a lot of different
areas within the project,

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.930
obviously, and I
oversee a lot of work.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.210
And I work at
Google, and you might

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.990
think that would be conflicting,
but I think it's not, really.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.800
The leads of the project
are very much fanatical

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.040
about being vendor
neutral, about being open,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.863
about being very transparent.

00:01:54.863 --> 00:01:57.030
The project is set up in a
way that everything we do

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.920
can be seen by anybody who
wants to pay attention.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.560
And I think that
shows in the quality

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.777
of the code, the quality
and number of contributors,

00:02:04.777 --> 00:02:05.860
where they're coming from.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.280
We have contributors from
every time zone on the planet,

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:09.630
from almost every continent.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.130
I still haven't proved whether
we have someone in Antarctica

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:12.810
or not.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.768
If you're in Antarctica
and you're contributing

00:02:14.768 --> 00:02:17.220
to Kubernetes, call me.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.870
This has been a
really important part.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.510
The vendors are a critical
piece of our ecosystem.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.950
Kubernetes would
not be successful

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.127
without the ecosystem.

00:02:24.127 --> 00:02:25.710
We knew this from
the beginning, and I

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.620
am happy to say that we're
really successful there.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.410
But it is still a
community-driven project

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.070
that is not tied or locked
in to any one vendor.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.870
While we have a ton
of people at Google

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.060
who are working on
Kubernetes, myself included,

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.390
they are not exclusively
focusing on Google's work.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.000
They're working to build
the project at large.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.860
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
you've got a lot of insight

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.758
with a lot of customers,
a lot of users,

00:02:49.758 --> 00:02:52.050
and a lot of community members
working with Kubernetes.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.660
What are some of the
trends you see emerging,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.620
maybe some unexpected trends
or patterns you've seen emerge?

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.520
TIM HOCKIN: So, I do.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.680
I talk to a lot of customers,
and I've definitely

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.480
seen some emerging trends.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.100
We've passed the
point where people

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.808
are struggling to figure
out if they need Kubernetes.

00:03:07.808 --> 00:03:10.350
It's more or less assumed now
if you're building big systems,

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.460
Kubernetes is the way to do it.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.150
But the patterns that are
emerging now are scale--

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:16.023
large size.

00:03:16.023 --> 00:03:17.190
How big can my clusters get?

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.620
How many pods can
I run on a node?

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.210
What's the density
that I can achieve?

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.520
How do I drive my utilization?

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.680
We're talking about
multi-cluster situations.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.930
I've got clusters around the
world, around the country--

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.530
how do I link them together
and make them work together?

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:32.947
Some of these
problems are sort of

00:03:32.947 --> 00:03:34.778
at the very edges of
Kubernetes itself,

00:03:34.778 --> 00:03:37.320
but I think there's a lot we
can do within Kubernetes to make

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.510
it easier to build
solutions that

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.130
are solving these problems.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.290
Hybrid is a really
hot topic right now.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.037
The reality of, you know, people
are stuck in other clouds,

00:03:46.037 --> 00:03:48.120
or they're stuck on-premises,
and there are really

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.280
good reasons for
that, and they're

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.410
trying to find ways to
work across these different

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:53.870
environments.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:55.620
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Tim,
thank you very much.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

